WEBVTT

00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:14,480
I'm sending it in a urine sample next week at the moment. I am not on an antibiotic

00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:21,760
for a minor UTI. Should I stop the antibiotic before sending it in the urine sample?

00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:28,960
No, no. So if you're doing a urine, it depends on what you're doing the urine sample for. So if you're

00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:39,680
doing a urine sample, um, well, I'm trying to think of what a person would, what a test urine sample test

00:00:40,640 --> 00:00:45,680
would make a difference if you're doing, if you're on an antibiotic and I don't think there is

00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:54,240
any. So even if you're doing a urine sample to test for a UTI, um, you still wouldn't stop an

00:00:54,240 --> 00:01:00,640
antibiotic because you're doing the antibiotic for a minor UTI. So you're trying to kill any bacteria

00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:07,120
into there. So no, no, no, no. You don't have to, you don't have to stop the urine. You don't have

00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:14,480
to stop the antibiotic for the urine sample for a microtox test, nor do you have to stop it for

00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:19,760
urine sample for a glyphosate test. If that's what you're doing, if it's the test that we sent you,

00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:26,960
then no, just do it. You do it tomorrow if you can, or whatever. Do it. The earliest

00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:37,680
convenience would be better. The antibiotic should not interfere with that.

